# Contributing to {{name}}

You do not need to know the whole project before contributing. This guide takes you from a fresh
fork to a pull request and shows how to use a coding assistant without letting it guess the
project's rules.

Read [LICENSE](LICENSE) before contributing; submitting a pull request accepts its contribution
terms.

## The instruction documents to know

- [README.md](README.md) explains what the mod does and how to build it.
- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) is an import-only manifest. Every `@path.md` listed there is part of the
  authoritative instruction set for coding assistants. Its first two imports,
  `docs/ai/shared/REPOSITORY.md` and `docs/ai/shared/PUBLIC-EVIDENCE.md`, are managed by mod-base.

The repository deliberately has no `CLAUDE.md`. Claude Code reads `AGENTS.md` directly, including
its imports, so Claude gets the same modular rules as other agents without a duplicate copy.
Adding a `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/CLAUDE.md` or `CLAUDE.local.md` would stop Claude from reading
`AGENTS.md`. Do not put rules directly in `AGENTS.md`.

## 1. Choose the correct base branch

All new changes target `{{canonical_branch}}`, including a fix for one Minecraft version or loader.
The release inventory named in `docs/ai/PROJECT.md` decides which versions and loaders are built;
never infer support from directory or branch names.

## 2. Prepare a checkout

Install Git, a JDK able to launch Gradle, and Python 3.11 or newer for the helper scripts. Fork the
repository on GitHub, clone your fork, add this repository as `upstream`, and create a topic
branch from `upstream/{{canonical_branch}}`:

```bash
git fetch upstream
git switch --create fix/short-description upstream/{{canonical_branch}}
```

If another task owns the current checkout, create a separate ephemeral worktree from the fetched
base instead. Remove it only after `git status --short` inside it is empty and every valuable
change belongs to a named, pushed branch; never pass `--force` to erase a dirty worktree.

## 3. Give an AI enough context

Coding agents should discover `AGENTS.md` automatically, but import behavior differs between tools.
Make the requirement explicit:

```text
Open AGENTS.md and read every @-imported Markdown file completely, then read CONTRIBUTING.md and
the focused documentation relevant to this task. Inspect git status and the release inventory
before editing. Explain the intended scope, make the smallest coherent change, preserve unrelated
work, do not edit generated output or mod-base managed files, and run proportional checks. Do not
commit, push, open a PR, weaken tests, or change the support inventory unless I ask.

Task: <describe one concrete bug or feature, including how to reproduce it>
```

AI output is not automatically correct. Read the diff, reject fixes that delete tests, relax
assertions, remove bounds or hide exceptions, and never paste tokens, credentials or private user
data into a prompt.

## 4. Know where changes belong

`docs/ai/PROJECT.md` maps each kind of change to its owning source. Generated output, build
directories, Minecraft runtime directories, screenshots and caches are never edited or committed.
Files managed by mod-base (the shared `docs/ai/shared/*.md`, `scripts/ci/mod_base_kit.py`,
`.gitattributes` and the managed region of `.github/workflows/pages.yml`) change only through the
kit: see step 5.

## 5. Run proportional checks

Run the smallest useful test while developing, then the repository-level checks before handing
work off:

```bash
git diff --check
{{test_command}}
python3 scripts/ci/mod_base_kit.py verify --network
python3 scripts/ci/mod_base_kit.py run template check --repo .
```

The first kit command fetches the pinned mod-base commit into your user cache (outside the
repository) when it is not there yet. Kit bumps are their own pull requests: run
`python3 scripts/ci/mod_base_kit.py bump --to vX.Y.Z` on a fresh branch and open it as a draft;
the bump rewrites every pin and re-synchronizes the managed files, and the complete gates run on
it like on any other change.

## 6. Review and commit

Keep one logical concern per commit, with a conventional, imperative subject (`feat:`, `fix:`,
`refactor:`, `test:`, `build:`, `ci:`, `docs:` or `chore:`). Review exactly what will be
committed:

```bash
git status --short
git diff --check
git add path/to/intended-file
git diff --cached --check
git diff --cached
git commit -m "fix: describe the actual change"
```

## 7. Update your branch safely

Before the first push, `git rebase upstream/{{canonical_branch}}` is fine; `git rebase --abort`
returns to the previous state. After a pull request is open, merge the base branch normally
instead of rewriting published history.

## 8. Open the pull request

Push the topic branch to your fork and open a pull request against `{{canonical_branch}}`. Use the
pull-request template: a reviewer must be able to see why the change is needed, which versions and
loaders it affects, which commands you ran and their outcomes, what you did not test, and whether
AI materially authored or reviewed the change. CI is part of the review: fix the cause of a
failure instead of weakening a gate.

## Getting help

Open an issue when the correct source owner or expected behavior is unclear. Include the version,
loader, reproduction steps and relevant logs.
